# without its tests, testdata, testing/ and http/e2e/ packages, docs, agent
# guides, or dev and CI files. scripts/update-coraza.sh copies a release or
# a commit on main the same way each time and applies scripts/coraza/*.patch
# in name order: upstream PRs not merged yet and our own fixes. A patch
# upstream took no longer applies and is deleted.
# govulncheck sees this copy without a version, so Coraza's advisories
# (github.com/corazawaf/coraza/security/advisories) are checked by hand.
#
# Changes, each marked "Changed by Ostiole" in the source:
# - internal/corazawaf/transaction.go: ctl:forceRequestBodyVariable=On
#   (CRS 901340) defaulted the body processor to URLENCODED for unknown
#   and binary Content-Types such as application/octet-stream, copying the
#   body into REQUEST_BODY and also parsing it into garbage ARGS_POST /
#   ARGS_POST_NAMES, which amplified CRS memory and CPU by ~10-100x.
#   Default to RAW so REQUEST_BODY is populated without bogus form args.
#   Upstream: issues #1628 and #1856, open at 1cfa4f9.
# - internal/transformations/css_decode.go: cssDecode only folded LF
#   after a backslash, leaving CR, CRLF and FF as literal characters so
#   payloads like aler\<CRLF>t(1) slipped past CRS 941xxx XSS rules.
#   Fold CR, CRLF and FF as CSS newlines and consume CRLF after hex escapes.
#   Upstream: PR #1677 (2026-08-02), approved and open at 1cfa4f9.
# - internal/corazawaf/rule.go, transaction.go and waf.go: RULE:msg and
#   RULE:logdata were macro-expanded for every evaluated rule rather than
#   at match time, chain-child loggers allocated with debug logging off,
#   stopWatches allocated a map per transaction, and Transaction.GetField
#   called strings.ToLower on every key even with no exceptions or plain
#   string exceptions (amplifying large keys by 3x on invalid UTF-8).
#   Expand RULE:msg/logdata on match, guard chain-child logging, use a
#   fixed [6]int64 stopWatches array, skip GetField filtering when there
#   are no exceptions, and compare plain exceptions with strings.EqualFold
#   while lowercasing lazily only for regex exceptions.
#   Upstream: PR #1750 (2026-10-12) and issue #1856, open at 1cfa4f9.
# - internal/corazawaf/body_buffer.go and transaction.go: bodyBufferReader
#   now implements io.WriterTo so body processors and audit logging read
#   in-memory bodies in one write without a 32 KiB scratch buffer, and
#   BodyBuffer.Reset drops buffers larger than 64 KiB instead of pinning
#   them in txPool.
#   Upstream: PR #1751 (2026-10-12), open at 1cfa4f9.
# - internal/collections/named.go: NamedCollectionNames.FindString looked
#   up keys without lowercasing, so ARGS_NAMES:Key with uppercase letters
#   silently never matched. Lowercase the key when the collection is
#   case-insensitive, matching Map.FindString.
#   Upstream: PR #1757 (2026-10-14), open at 1cfa4f9.
# - internal/bodyprocessors/multipart.go: parts were read with NextPart,
#   which decodes a part sent with Content-Transfer-Encoding
#   quoted-printable and hides that header. Backends are handed the raw
#   bytes, so a payload written with =XX escapes or soft line breaks got
#   past rules that match the raw form. Parts are read with NextRawPart.
#   Upstream: PR #1759 (2026-10-14), open at 1cfa4f9.
# - internal/corazawaf/transaction.go: RESPONSE_CONTENT_TYPE took the
#   last Content-Type header untrimmed and IsResponseBodyProcessable
#   matched SecResponseBodyMimeType case-sensitively, so responses with
#   Text/HTML or trailing spaces skipped phase 4 inspection. Keep the
#   first trimmed Content-Type and compare with strings.EqualFold.
#   Upstream: PR #1765 (2026-10-15), open at 1cfa4f9.
# - internal/collections, internal/bodyprocessors, internal/cookies,
#   internal/url, internal/corazawaf and http: collections were Go maps, so
#   ARGS, cookies, headers and their _NAMES came out in a new order on every
#   evaluation, and a chain reading its parent's last match (TX:0-9,
#   MATCHED_VAR) could deny a request on one transaction and pass it on the
#   next (CRS 942130, 942131, 942521). A Map keeps its keys in insertion
#   order, and the parsers add in input order. coraza-caddy feeds headers
#   itself, from a map, so header order under it is still unsorted.
#   Upstream: issue #1815 and PR #1834 (2026-10-26), open at 1cfa4f9.
# - internal/collections/map.go: Map.Reset cleared index and entries in
#   place, keeping peak map buckets and slice capacity in txPool after an
#   argument-heavy request. Drop and rebuild past 256 keys (PR #1751
#   adapted to PR #1834's ordered map layout).
#   Upstream: PR #1751 (2026-10-12) on top of PR #1834, open at 1cfa4f9.
# - internal/actions/ctl.go, internal/corazawaf/rulegroup.go, transaction.go
#   and waf.go, internal/seclang/directives.go: ctl:ruleRemoveTargetById,
#   ByTag and ByMsg, and ctl:ruleRemoveByTag and ByMsg, walked every rule by
#   value on every request, and a target removal then added an entry per
#   selected rule, one allocation each. ctl:ruleRemoveTargetByTag=OWASP_CRS
#   selects every CRS rule, which cost 68 us and 720 allocations a request. A
#   ctl caches the IDs it selects per version of the rule group, which every
#   added, removed or in-place edited rule changes, and the removals share one
#   capped slice: 24 us and 118 allocations. Rules without an id are no longer
#   touched by ctl removals: all share ID 0, so one removal hit them all and
#   skipped every SecMarker (the ctl part of upstream PR #1782).
#   Upstream: issue #1848 and PR #1849 (2026-11-08), open at 1cfa4f9.
# - internal/corazawaf/rulegroup.go and transaction.go: empty the
#   transformation cache at the end of each phase instead of the start of
#   the next so phase-2 transformed strings are not held in memory while
#   waiting on upstreams or open WebSockets, and replace the map past
#   8192 entries so oversized bucket arrays are not retained in txPool.
#   Upstream: PR #1865 (2026-11-14), open at 1cfa4f9.
